  "infoWhatIsWalletTitle": "What a wallet is (and is not)",
  "infoWhatIsWalletBody": "A wallet does not ''contain'' coins: it holds the private keys that let you spend coins recorded on the blockchain. Nodes calculate the balance by reading the chain, and the app rebuilds it from your addresses. That is why losing the keys means losing the funds, while losing the phone can be recovered with the 12-word phrase.",
  "infoSelfCustodyTitle": "Self-custody: nobody can freeze your funds",
  "infoSelfCustodyBody": "Here you are the bank: no account, no login, no company holding your money. The upside: nobody can freeze your balance. The risk: nobody can recover your password or your recovery phrase. That is the price of sovereignty — accept it knowingly.",
  "infoSeedPhraseTitle": "Recovery phrase (12 words)",
  "infoSeedPhraseBody": "It is the human-readable form of your private key: those words rebuild ALL your addresses, and therefore your funds. Write it on paper, never as a photo, in chats, in the cloud or in a password manager. Whoever reads it can empty the wallet; whoever loses it has lost everything.",
  "infoAppEncryptionTitle": "Local encryption and password",
  "infoAppEncryptionBody": "The phrase stays on your device encrypted with AES-GCM: the key is derived from your password (or from your fingerprint, which unlocks the system keystore). No server ever receives the password. If you forget it there is no ''reset'': the recovery phrase is the only way back.",
  "infoNetworkBlake2bTitle": "The network: bitcoin-blake2b",
  "infoNetworkBlake2bBody": "You are using bitcoin-blake2b, an EXPERIMENTAL network with its own rules (BLAKE2b hashing, no consensus-level replay protection): it is not Bitcoin and the two chains do not share coins. Treat this wallet as a test environment, not as your final savings vault.",
  "infoBalanceUtxoTitle": "How the balance is built",
  "infoBalanceUtxoBody": "There is no ''account'' with a number: the balance is the sum of UTXOs, the coins you received and have not spent yet — like banknotes in a pocket. A transaction consumes some UTXOs and creates new ones (payment + change). Sending to yourself leaves the balance unchanged.",
  "infoConfirmationsTitle": "Confirmations",
  "infoConfirmationsBody": "A new payment is ''pending'' (mempool): it exists, but no block has recorded it yet. Every later block adds a confirmation; from 3 confirmations on a payment is reasonably stable. While pending, the amount may show as unavailable: it has not vanished, it is waiting.",
  "infoFeeConceptTitle": "Why you pay a fee",
  "infoFeeConceptBody": "The fee is the reward for whoever includes your transaction in a block. You pay per size (sat/vB), not per amount: sending 1,000 or 1,000,000 sat costs the same. When the mempool is busy prices rise; the fee is NOT an app fee — it goes to the miners.",
  "infoChangeAddressTitle": "Change",
  "infoChangeAddressBody": "Like at the shop: if the UTXOs you spend are worth more than the amount, the difference comes back to you on a NEW address of the same wallet (the ''change''). Seeing a transaction with two outputs is normal: the payment to the recipient and the change to you.",
  "infoLightningVsOnchainTitle": "On-chain and Lightning",
  "infoLightningVsOnchainBody": "On-chain means every movement is a public transaction on the chain: safe and verifiable, but slower and with a fee per transaction. Lightning means instant, almost free payments inside ''channels'' opened with one on-chain transaction. Opening a channel moves on-chain funds into the channel; closing it moves them back on-chain.",
  "infoPrivacyBasicsTitle": "Privacy: who sees what",
  "infoPrivacyBasicsBody": "Every ''Receive'' creates a new address: reusing one links your payments together. The app has no account and no telemetry, and asks a public API (mempool.guide) for on-chain data — that API sees your IP. For maximum privacy use a VPN or your own node, and never publish your xpub.",
  "infoCoinControlTitle": "Coin control (UTXO selection)",
  "infoCoinControlBody": "Your balance is made of UTXOs, the pieces you received. Here you can pick which ones to spend: the transaction will use only those, letting you keep small or inactive pieces aside.",
  "infoDustLimitTitle": "Minimum amount (dust)",
  "infoDustLimitBody": "Outputs below 546 sat are rejected by the network as 'dust'. Amounts under that limit cannot be sent.",
  "infoFeeRateTitle": "Transaction fee",
  "infoFeeRateBody": "The fee is paid per unit of transaction size (sat/vB): the faster you want the confirmation, the more you pay. 'Economical' can take hours, 'Priority' a few minutes. 'Custom' is for when you know the current mempool rate.",
  "infoBatchSendTitle": "Multiple recipients (batch)",
  "infoBatchSendBody": "In a single transaction you can pay up to 20 addresses, sharing the fee instead of paying it once per transfer. All recipients are shown in the confirmation before signing.",
  "infoBumpFeeTitle": "Increase fee (RBF)",
  "infoBumpFeeBody": "A pending transaction can be replaced by a new one paying a higher fee (BIP125). The original is cancelled and only the replacement can confirm — the destination address and amount stay the same.",
  "infoXpubTitle": "Account public key (xpub)",
  "infoXpubBody": "The xpub generates all your receiving addresses. It cannot move funds, but it reveals the whole balance and history: share it only with apps you trust (e.g. a watch-only wallet).",
  "infoReceiveAddressTitle": "Receiving address",
  "infoReceiveAddressBody": "Each 'Receive' shows a fresh address, chosen from those never used: this keeps payments unlinkable. Reusing an address is not an error, it just makes your transactions easier to trace.",
  "infoWatchOnlyTitle": "Watch-only wallet",
  "infoWatchOnlyBody": "You imported only the xpub: the app sees balance and history but holds no private key, so it cannot sign. To spend from this wallet you need the device that holds the seed.",
  "infoSignVerifyTitle": "Sign / verify message",
  "infoSignVerifyBody": "Signing proves that an address is yours without moving funds. Anyone can then verify the signature against that address and the same message.",
  "infoChannelCapacityTitle": "Channel capacity",
  "infoChannelCapacityBody": "The total amount of satoshis in the channel, split between you and your peer. The more capacity, the larger payments it can handle. Capacity = local balance + remote balance.",
  "infoChannelReserveTitle": "Channel reserve",
  "infoChannelReserveBody": "A small portion of your funds must stay locked as a security deposit (the 'reserve'). It ensures both parties have skin in the game — if the other side goes offline maliciously, the reserve can be used to penalize them on-chain.",
  "infoToSelfDelayTitle": "To-self delay",
  "infoToSelfDelayBody": "When a force close happens, your on-chain output is delayed by this many blocks (typically 144 = ~1 day). This gives your peer time to claim their funds first, preventing double-spend attacks on the channel state.",
  "infoHtlcTitle": "HTLC (Hashed Time-Locked Contract)",
  "infoHtlcBody": "An HTLC is a conditional payment: funds are locked until the recipient reveals a secret hash preimage. In Lightning, HTLCs enable instant off-chain routing — your payment hops through multiple channels without trusting any intermediary.",
  "infoOpenChannelPrivateTitle": "Private channel",
  "infoOpenChannelPrivateBody": "A private channel is not announced to the network. Only you and your peer know it exists. Use it when you don''t want others to route through it (privacy) or when the channel is too small to be useful for routing.",
  "infoRoutingFeesTitle": "Routing fees",
  "infoRoutingFeesBody": "When other nodes route payments through your channel, you earn fees. Base fee (sat) is charged per payment; rate (ppm) is proportional to the amount. CLTV delta limits how long a forwarded HTLC can take to settle.",
  "infoForceCloseTitle": "Force close",
  "infoForceCloseBody": "Broadcasts your latest channel state on-chain. This is irreversible and requires waiting for the to-self delay before you can spend your funds. Use only if your peer is unresponsive or malicious — cooperative close is always faster and cheaper.",
  "infoPeersTitle": "Connected peers",
  "infoPeersBody": "Peers are other Lightning nodes you''re directly connected to via TCP/Tor. Each peer can hold one or more channels. You can connect to new peers to open channels and increase your node''s liquidity and routing capability.",
  "infoNodeManagementTitle": "Node management",
  "infoNodeManagementBody": "Your Lightning node identity: pubkey, version, number of active/pending channels and peers. This screen shows accounting data from the node''s bookkeeper plugin and forwarding statistics.",
